23:28:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Even if your thermostat is set to a steady 72°F year-round, you can feel noticeably colder in winter due to a combination of physics and physiology: - **Radiant heat loss to cold surfaces** – Your body constantly exchanges heat with surrounding surfaces (walls, windows, floors). In winter, those surfaces are much colder than the air, so you radiate more body heat toward them, making you feel chilly even though the air temperature is 72°F. This is the “mean radiant temperature” effect. - **Lower indoor humidity** – Cold outside air holds very little moisture. When it’s heated indoors, the relative humidity plummets (often below 30%). Dry air accelerates evaporation of moisture from your skin, which cools you just like sweating. At the same air temperature, lower humidity can feel perceptibly cooler. - **Drafts and air leaks** – Tiny air leaks around windows and doors can create local air movement across your skin, increasing convective heat loss. Even a slight draft at 72°F can make you feel several degrees colder. - **Physiological acclimatization** – Your body gradually adapts to seasonal temperatures. In winter, your skin and blood-flow patterns are used to cooler conditions, so a “neutral” 72°F can feel cooler than the same temperature would in summer. - **Clothing differences** – In summer, you’re probably wearing lighter, more breathable clothes, while in winter you might be in long sleeves but still have exposed skin (hands, face) that loses heat in a room that feels thermally “colder” due to the factors above. In short, the thermostat only reads air temperature, but your comfort depends on the total heat balance: radiation, convection, humidity, and your own physiology all shift in winter, making 72°F **feel** colder.
